speechtext:
In a general way, the
following is a detailed estimate of the expenditure:	Royal Naval College, $90,000;
dockyard at Halifax, $200,000; dockyard at Esquimalt, $85,000; Royal Naval Canadian Volunteer Reserve, $200,000; general amount for salaries at headquarters, pensions, contributions to Imperial Government, and miscellaneous expenses, $50,000; amount available for the Niobe, Rainbow, submarines, etc., $375,000. The amount expended last year was $512,805.66. Up to the 31st of January of the current year, we have expended $386,440.31. A large amount of this expenditure comes out of the war appropriation, and it might therefore be argued that this vote might very fairly be reduced by a considerable amount. The difficulty about that, however, is as I pointed out last year, that if the war should cease, and these different services had to be carried on, we are advised that we would have no right then to draw upon the war vote for that purpose. It is for that reason that we are asking for $1,000,000. If the war goes on, I presume the amount spent will not be larger than it was ,in 1914-15, amounting to about $500,000. With the war on, the expenses of keeping up these ships, which are placed at the disposal of the Imperial Government, are paid for by the war vote, and do not come out of the ordinary appropriation voted by this Parliament for the ordinary expenditures. That is the reason why such a considerable portion of the amount was not spent. Unless we have the vote, we would, if the war should cease, find ourselves in very considerable difficulty in getting it for the purpose of carrying on the service.
